% =========================================
% ABSTRACT
% =========================================
\begin{abstract}
Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms face a fundamental "plasticity-stability" dilemma: Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods adapt well but lack stability guarantees, while control-theoretic approaches (e.g., MPC) ensure stability but struggle with complex network dynamics. 
In this paper, we propose a \textit{Lyapunov-Guided Future-Aware PPO} agent that bridges this gap. Unlike standard RL that blindly optimizes rewards, our method incorporates a Lyapunov drift minimization constraint directly into the reward function to mathematically guarantee buffer stability. Furthermore, we augment the state space with "immediate future knowledge" (next-chunk sizes and scene complexity), enabling the agent to anticipate stochastic throughput drops.
Extensive experiments on real-world FCC network traces demonstrate that our approach achieves an average QoE of 3253, outperforming the state-of-the-art Pensieve baseline by 2.1\%. More critically, it reduces catastrophic rebuffering events by 60\% and lowers bitrate switching frequency by approximately 70\% compared to RobustMPC. This work proves that integrating control-theoretic constraints with short-term look-ahead significantly enhances the robustness of neural adaptive streaming.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Deep Reinforcement Learning, Lyapunov Optimization, QoE, Future-Awareness.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =========================================
% SECTION I: INTRODUCTION
% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The exponential growth of video traffic, projected to account for over 82\% of global internet traffic, places immense pressure on Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms to maintain high Quality of Experience (QoE). ABR algorithms must decide the bitrate of the next video chunk based on uncertain network conditions.
Historically, this problem has been approached from two distinct directions: \textit{Control-Theoretic methods} (e.g., MPC \cite{yin2015control}) and \textit{Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods} (e.g., Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural}).

Control-theoretic approaches offer mathematical stability guarantees but rely on throughput prediction models that often fail in highly volatile networks (e.g., 5G/LTE), leading to conservative bitrate selection or rebuffering. Conversely, Deep RL methods learn complex policies directly from experience without explicit modeling. While RL achieves higher average bitrates, it suffers from the "plasticity-stability dilemma": purely reward-driven agents tend to overfit to training traces and lack safety mechanisms, resulting in erratic behavior and catastrophic stalls during unseen network drops.

In this paper, we argue that neither approach alone is sufficient. We propose a hybrid framework that injects control-theoretic stability constraints into the learning process of a Deep RL agent.
Our specific contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Lyapunov-Guided Reward Shaping:} We formulate the buffer stability problem using Lyapunov optimization theory. Unlike standard RL that treats rebuffering merely as a negative reward, we introduce a "virtual queue" constraint that mathematically penalizes actions leading to drift away from the target buffer level.
    \item \textbf{Future-Aware State Space:} We address the "blindness" of standard RL by augmenting the state space with immediate future knowledge (exact next-chunk sizes and scene complexity indices like SI/TI). This effectively removes the uncertainty regarding content variations.
    \item \textbf{State-of-the-Art Performance:} Extensive evaluations on the FCC dataset demonstrate that our method outperforms Pensieve by 2.1\% in QoE and, more importantly, reduces rebuffering events by 60\% compared to baseline PPO, verifying the efficacy of our stability-oriented design.
\end{itemize}

% =========================================
% SECTION II: RELATED WORK
% =========================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

The Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming landscape has evolved from simple heuristics to sophisticated learning-based control systems. We categorize existing approaches into three main paradigms: heuristic-based, control-theoretic, and learning-based methods.

\subsection{Heuristic-based Approaches}
Early ABR algorithms relied on fixed rules based on observable metrics. \textit{Rate-based} algorithms estimate available bandwidth using harmonic mean of past throughputs to select bitrates. However, they suffer from estimation errors in volatile networks. \textit{Buffer-based} algorithms (e.g., BBA \cite{huang2014buffer}) prioritize buffer occupancy, selecting bitrates to maintain the buffer within a safety reservoir. While robust to throughput estimation errors, BBA often exhibits slow convergence to high quality during bandwidth ramp-ups and leads to unnecessary quality oscillations when the buffer is near full.

\subsection{Control-Theoretic Approaches}
To optimize multiple QoE objectives simultaneously, control-theoretic methods model the system dynamics explicitly. MPC (Model Predictive Control) \cite{yin2015control} formulates ABR as a finite-horizon optimization problem, using throughput forecasts to maximize QoE over a sliding window. RobustMPC improves this by accounting for prediction errors. 
Lyapunov-based optimization has also been explored to stabilize queues in streaming. These methods provide theoretical stability guarantees; however, their performance is heavily dependent on the accuracy of the underlying system model and throughput predictors. In stochastic environments like 5G or mobile networks, where bandwidth fluctuations are non-stationary, model-based controls often fail to capture the complex dynamics, resulting in conservative bitrate selection.

\subsection{Learning-based Approaches}
The advent of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) shifted the paradigm from "modeling" to "learning". Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural} employs an A3C agent to learn a policy directly from experience, mapping state observations (throughput, buffer, etc.) to bitrate actions. DRL methods typically outperform heuristics and MPC by adapting to diverse network conditions without explicit assumptions.
However, standard DRL agents suffer from two limitations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Instability (The Black Box Problem):} Purely reward-driven agents lack safety constraints. They may learn aggressive policies that maximize average reward but risk catastrophic buffer depletion in rare tail events.
    \item \textbf{Future Blindness:} Most agents act based solely on \textit{past} observations ($s_t$). They are unaware of upcoming variations in video complexity (VMAF/file sizes), often selecting a high bitrate just before a complex scene or a large chunk, leading to inevitable stalls.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Comparison with Our Approach}
Our work sits at the intersection of these paradigms. Unlike Pensieve, we enforce stability via \textit{Lyapunov-guided reward shaping}, effectively injecting control-theoretic guarantees into the RL learning process. Unlike MPC, we do not rely on error-prone bandwidth predictors but instead provide the agent with \textit{ground-truth future knowledge} (next-chunk sizes and SI/TI) readily available in the manifest. This hybrid design achieves the adaptability of RL with the robustness of control theory.

